{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792398082,"nonce":"mAcl+BQOpdv6sgL3kesR1GHIdFI=","title":"LSP must work on any Haxe project configured by .hxml, not only rayzor.toml","message":"User requirement (2026-10-19): the language server is not rayzor-specific; it must serve any Haxe codebase configured the standard way, through an .hxml file.\n\nToday `LspContext::load_workspace_config` (`lsp/src/context.rs:37`) reads only `rayzor.toml`. A plain Haxe project gets no class paths, libraries or defines, so imports outside the opened file do not resolve and `#if` branches are evaluated with the wrong defines.\n\nNeeded:\n- Discovery: find `*.hxml` in the workspace root (`build.hxml` first; ask the client or take an `initializationOptions.hxml` / configuration setting when there are several). Read `rayzor.toml` only when there is no hxml or it names one.\n- Apply the hxml:\n  - `-cp`/`--class-path`/`-p` become class paths;\n  - `-lib`/`--library` are resolved through haxelib (`haxelib path` or the `.haxelib` repo layout, honouring `HAXELIB_PATH` and a local `.haxelib`);\n  - `-D`/`--define` become defines;\n  - `-main`/`--main` sets the entry point;\n  - the target flag (`--js`, `--cpp`, `--hl`, `--jvm`, `--interp`, ...) sets the target's own defines (`js`, `cpp`, `sys`...) so conditional compilation matches what haxe would compile;\n  - `--macro` calls are run, or reported as unsupported.\n- Multi-config files (`--next`, `--each`) give one analysis context per section; a file belongs to the context whose class paths contain it.\n- Files outside every class path still get single-file analysis.\n- Re-read the hxml when it changes (see 9255a45a4b05b928151aa33bdca52a5d561aa955fc58e6bc8cfa7e17c98bb058 for watched files).\n\nParser gaps are tracked separately.","files":null}]}